    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Absolutely, let's dissect the distinction. If you write a specific, unique algorithm as part of a project, this would most certainly be considered 'Confidential Information'— it is a proprietary creation and integral to the project. The laws and NDA provisions often protect such intellectual property created during employment or upon specific contractual agreement.\n\nOn the contrary, 'Residuals' such as the general approach or methodology towards problem-solving or the logical way in which you tackle coding tasks are personal knowledge and are not proprietary to your employer. These iterations of skills, gained through experience and cannot be reduced to tangible documentation, often remain with you. However, crossing the line comes into play if you repeat the exact code or the specific methods that are the company's intellectual property. The key is the difference between applying the general knowledge that you possess and memorizing or reusing your employer’s unique, work-related intellectual creations. It is a nuanced, albeit important, distinction. attorney consultation would help you to draw that line based on the definite terms of your NDA and the context of how such residual knowledge might apply to your work post-employment or post-contract.",
